"The key tells React which item is which between renders. If I use the index and the list is reordered or an item is removed, React mixes up the rows and their state, such as an input's text. I use a stable id from the data."

```jsx
{tareas.map((tarea) => (
  <Tarea key={tarea.id} tarea={tarea} />
))}
```

"`useState` gives a component memory: it returns the current value and a setter. Calling the setter does not change the variable right away; it requests a new render, and in that render the value is the new one. That is why, when the next value depends on the previous one, I use the functional form."

```jsx
const [cuenta, setCuenta] = useState(0);

function sumarTres() {
  setCuenta((c) => c + 1);
  setCuenta((c) => c + 1);
  setCuenta((c) => c + 1); // termina en 3; con setCuenta(cuenta + 1) terminaría en 1
}
```

"It synchronizes the component with something outside React: a subscription, a timer, the document title. The dependency array says when to re-run it: empty means only on mount; with values, when they change. If I return a function, React runs it to clean up before re-running the effect and on unmount."

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => setSegundos((s) => s + 1), 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id); // limpieza
}, []);
```

"When I can compute something during render. With a list and a filter, I compute the filtered list directly; I do not store it in another state with an effect, because that adds a render and a risk of getting out of sync. Nor for responding to a click: that belongs in the event handler."

```jsx
// Bien: valor derivado, sin efecto ni estado extra
const visibles = tareas.filter((t) => t.titulo.includes(busqueda));
```

"A custom hook is a function whose name starts with `use` and that calls other hooks. It lets me reuse stateful logic across components without repeating code. For example, one that tells whether the user is online."

```jsx
function useEnLinea() {
  const [enLinea, setEnLinea] = useState(true);
  useEffect(() => {
    const actualizar = () => setEnLinea(navigator.onLine);
    actualizar();
    window.addEventListener("online", actualizar);
    window.addEventListener("offline", actualizar);
    return () => {
      window.removeEventListener("online", actualizar);
      window.removeEventListener("offline", actualizar);
    };
  }, []);
  return enLinea;
}
```

"Two things. One, accessing a DOM element, for example to focus an input. Two, holding a value that must survive between renders but should not trigger a render when it changes, like a timer id."

```jsx
const campo = useRef(null);
useEffect(() => { campo.current?.focus(); }, []);
return <input ref={campo} />;
```

"In a client app I use TanStack Query: it handles loading, errors, caching and retries, and saves me from hand-written effects. In Next.js I fetch on the server inside Server Components, so the HTML arrives with the content. I use a raw `useEffect` only for very simple cases, making sure to abort the request."

```jsx
const { data, isPending, error } = useQuery({
  queryKey: ["noticias"],
  queryFn: () => fetch("/api/noticias").then((r) => r.json()),
});
```
